@Peter -- And thanks to you for the link.  I didn't know much about the history of the Polo Grounds prior to the 1950s Giants -- and I found this article interesting.  And not just as a baseball fan either.  The part about the owner completely rebuilding the stadium in steel and concrete IN ONLY 5 OR 6 WEEKS!!!! -- We knew how to build stuff back then!

FWIW, I'm sitting a block from 94th and Third Avenue, where a huge opening has been yawning in the street for about two years now -- topped by a huge yellow temporary plastic (I think) covering that's the length and width of the street.  Sometimes the opening is opened; mostly it's not.  There's a huge piece of equipment hovering over it all.  Usually it's running -- gushing noise and fumes.  But sometimes it's just sitting there.  Meanwhile there are 6-8 workmen there every day but Sunday -- waving flags to direct cars and bikes to take a detour that the pedestrian can barely see or figure out.  I will not cross Third Avenue at this crossing -- ever.   What are they doing there?  NOBODY SEEMS TO KNOW WHAT THE BLEEP THEY ARE DOING THERE!  Mostly the workers shrug or ignore you.  The closest thing I got to an answer were the words "lots of wires down there" and "very, very dangerous".  

As I say, about two years and counting.  Meanwhile, the owner of the Giants rebuilt the Polo Grounds in 5 or 6 weeks!  Were we a great country back then or what?

